A||N||9||kornholio||raising vehicle for alternator replacement||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||09-22-1999||04:20 PM||||Is it absolutely neccessary?  I was thinking of replacing it myself, but I don't have any jack stands.||||reg||1
Z||000001||88 Fiero||09-22-1999||04:52 PM||||If you have a V6 it's a must that you jack up the car...That's the only way of replacing the alternator....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||09-22-1999||05:42 PM||||If you drive the right wheels up onto a high curb you just might be able to get under the car enough to change out the altenator.  It's not a hard job if you have a standard transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dans87gt||09-22-1999||06:07 PM||||I changed mine on the side of the road on I-95, it was not fun. I did remove the passenger rear tire and mud guard to gain access.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||09-22-1999||07:44 PM||||How important is it to adjust the belt to the correct tension? Is there a way to adjust it without a gauge? I think I'm going to try to change the alternator myself by putting the rear wheels on ramps since they are far cheaper than jack stands.  Also, does the 2 pin connector that plugs into the alternator connect only one way?  Could I accidentily reverse them?  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000005||mwbackus||09-22-1999||08:10 PM||||If you have a manual trans on a later model Fiero it is quite simple to r&r the alt.  I was able to do this without jacking the car at all.  I use a coat hanger to lower the old one out and raise the new one into position.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||kornholio||09-22-1999||09:13 PM||||Mine is an automatic V6.  I also noticed that there is a metal plate over the alternator.  Should I transfer this plate over to the new alternator?  What's this plate for?||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||09-22-1999||09:54 PM||||That metal thing partially covering your alternator is a heat shield. I recommend putting it back on when you install your new alternator. You don't want the belt too tight (that's hard on alt. bearings), but too loose is also very bad (the alt. slips and wears out the belt faster). After the belt is tightened, I use my thumb to push on the belt. If I can move it about 1/2 inch, that's about right. Anymore than 1 inch is too loose.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JetroGT||09-23-1999||12:23 AM||||You can get the alternator out the top. But first you have to take off the exhaust manifold. I did it that way once because I didn't know any better.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||09-23-1999||01:52 AM||||<BR>Just get underneath the car and do it. A little dirt in the eyes never hurt anyone. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||6
